Key Strength
Uses precipitation only; can characterize drought or abnormal wetness at different time scales which correspond with the time availability of different water resources (e.g. soil moisture, snowpack, groundwater, river discharge and reservoir storage)
More comparable across regions with different climates than the Palmer Severity Drought Index (PDSI)
Less complex to calculate than the PDSI

Key Limitations
As a measure of water supply only, the SPI does not account for evapotranspiration, and this limits its ability to capture the effect of increased temperatures (associated with climate change) on moisture demand and availability
Sensitive to the quantity and reliability of the data used to fit the distribution; 30-50 years recommended
Does not consider the intensity of precipitation and its potential impacts on runoff, streamflow, and water availability within the system of interest
Since temeprature is not taken into account, interpret carefully over long time scales.

source: https://climatedataguide.ucar.edu/climate-data/standardized-precipitation-index-spi', NULL, NULL, 'https://github.com/mintproject/MINT-WorkflowDomain/raw/master/WINGSWorkflowComponents/dsi-1.0.0/dsi-1.0.0.zip', 'https://github.com/mintproject/MINT-WorkflowDomain/raw/master/WINGSWorkflowComponents/dsi-1.0.0/src/run', 'https://w3id.org/okn/i/mint/MINTPROJECT-DROUGHTINDICES%253ALATEST', NULL, 'https://w3id.org/okn/i/mint/Ethiopia', NULL, NULL, 'Expert-configured', NULL, NULL) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

At its core, GeoFlood couples two powerful components: the GeoNet algorithm for extracting high-fidelity river networks from LiDAR-derived Digital Elevation Models (DEMs), and the Height Above Nearest Drainage (HAND) method, which transforms terrain elevation data into relative height maps suitable for inundation estimation. By calculating the elevation difference between land surface cells and their nearest drainage paths, GeoFlood can infer potential water depth and flood extent for a given discharge scenario.

Unlike computationally intensive hydrodynamic models, GeoFlood uses synthetic rating curves derived from terrain and estimated channel roughness, enabling rapid conversion of forecasted river flows into inundation maps without sacrificing spatial detail. The system supports reach-averaged hydraulic parameterization using constant-length stream segments, improving consistency and resolution compared to traditional river network datasets.

Developed and validated using the Onion Creek watershed in Central Texas, GeoFlood demonstrated strong alignment with FEMA floodplain maps (60–90% overlap), while significantly reducing computation time. Its performance is especially effective in rural, fluvial flood-prone areas with well-defined topography. The software leverages parallel terrain processing tools such as TauDEM and integrates seamlessly with GIS workflows, making it highly suitable for large-scale, real-time, or ensemble flood forecasting applications.

GeoFlood is ideal for agencies, researchers, and planners who need rapid, approximate flood extents using high-resolution topography—whether for emergency response, climate resilience planning, or hydrologic analysis in ungauged basins.', 'flood inundation mapping, high-resolution terrain analysis, LiDAR-derived topography, digital elevation models, DEM, Height Above Nearest Drainage, HAND, synthetic rating curves, simplified hydraulic modeling, real-time flood mapping, channel network extraction, GeoFlood workflow, GeoNet channel extraction, geodesic least-cost path, D-infinity flow direction, pit-filling, flow accumulation, parallel hydrological terrain analysis, nonlinear multiscale filtering, riverine flood modeling,', NULL, 'https://portals.tapis.io/v3/apps/Pygeoflood-Container-wm/0.0.55sha-7b65417', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

